School 

When Paul came back for junior year he was focused. Focused on pool. 

He tried going to lectures, at least the physics lectures but when his professor would turn his back to the class and literally read from the book he lost interest quickly. He tried doing the home work, at least in his physics, but when the homework was boring, repetitive and didn’t teach him anything new, he lost interest in that too. 

He went to classes that graded attendance and he showed up for tests. He studied too but only for the tests. He would crack the book the night before take the test and typically set the curve. 

His grades suffered a little for not doing the homework and he took a lot of flack from TAs for skipping classes but he was still a solid B and that was good enough for what it was. 

He played pool every chance he got. He was at the Union hall when it opened, until it closed. He was just starting to realize how amazing a hall it really was. He hadn’t really been to a real pool hall before coming to school but now after having been to a couple dozen he knew that this room was special. It had twenty four tables. Twenty one nine foot pool tables, two nine foot snooker tables and one nine foot three cushion billiard table.  

The cloth was changed by professionals every summer while the students were away. So when he came back to school, it was to a room full of new cloth. The room had a two story ceiling with perfect fluorescent lighting. There was never a flickering bulb or it was replaced immediately. The light was like being outside on a day with perfect light cloud cover. Just bright enough without being too bright. There were never any shadows on the tables. 

What a contrast to the pool halls he had been in! 

And the entrance was perfect. It sat in the hallway between the student union and the library. The room was down two flights of stairs from the hallway with a landing and a right turn between them. And there at the landing where you looked directly at it coming down the stairs was a glass case with notices of upcoming events, and of course, a ten by twelve picture of last years school ACUI tournament champion. Paul’s picture. 

There was a row of windows all along the hallway above. Paul loved to just stand there and watch from above. It looked like a football field and you were in the crowd watching them play. But this wasn’t a coarse brutish game like football where the only significant thinking was done by the coach. This was pool; a game of finesse, strategy and deep thinking. Psychological mastery of your body and your mind. A game with such finesse that millimeters mattered in how you struck the ball and sometimes a millimeter difference in the position of the cue ball could win or lose the game.

